turning to our reporting on resilience  this morning we meet a couple determined  to realize our lifelong dream of owning  their own business when their new york  restaurant was hit hard by the pandemic  the couple refused to close their doors  and kept going with the power of their  own determination our senior medical  correspondent dr tara narula is here  with their story of resilience along  with some key strategies to help us all  get through challenging times tara good  morning good morning vlad gloribel perez  and walid marabit were small business  owners when the pandemic threatened to  close the doors of their restaurant what  kept them going was a resilience they  say they never knew they had but experts  say it was there all along  we're going to make it happen  you know it for gloribel perez and her  husband waleed murabit owning a business  was their american dream  in october of 2019 their restaurant  barcha was born  [Applause]  this restaurant was your dream yeah as  someone who  chose this country who immigrated here  and to be able to make something and  build something with my life partner  it's  it just meant everything and the couple  was overjoyed when only four months  after their grand opening their latin  fused labor of love was quickly becoming  a favorite in their east harlem  neighborhood  and then covet hit your whole world  turns upside down yeah absolutely it was  a bit of a spiral of despair for a bit  there  although i didn't have all the  information i needed at the time and i  still don't i knew how to figure it out  to stay afloat the couple made some big  adjustments including shifting to an  online business and partnering with  rethink a company that provides support  to restaurants in food and secure  communities what do you think it was in  your backgrounds that gave you that  attitude of we have to figure out life  we have to pivot and problem solve i  think the totality of my lived  experience has made me resilient and i  think this pandemic has through no  choice of our own has made us all  resilient as well adversity  doesn't  discriminate most experts would agree  that we are all actually hardwired to  overcome life's biggest challenges  do you think that we as a society  recognize how resilient we are i think  sadly a lot of people think of it as  some kind of elusive trait that some  people have and other people don't  the normal response is actually  resilience lucy hohn is the co-director  at the new zealand institute of  well-being and resilience her ted talk  one of the most viewed of 2020  highlights the three secrets of  resilient people so number one for  starters learning to accept that  struggle and challenge are a part of  everyday life they are universal so that  when something goes wrong  this stops you from feeling  discriminated against it stops you from  feeling singled out honest book  resilient grieving was born out of her  own personal tragedy when in 2014 her 12  year old daughter abby was killed in a  car accident i literally went from being  this supposed resilient expert to  my whole life smashed to smithereens  really amidst her grief hon zeroed in on  specific ways of thinking she says are  critical to helping us navigate the most  difficult times in our lives resilient  people are really good at focusing their  attention on the things  that they can change  and somehow accepting  the things that they can't change and  her third and final strategy one she  calls self-regulation focus on positive  thinking and living the people who  manage to cope  with really terrible things  very often have a real sense  of personal  mission and when life gets in the way  says hohn find a way to chart a new  course for gloribel perez and walid  marabit having adapted to a new business  model providing meals for their food  insecure neighbors their mission now is  giving back to the community they love  one meal at a time i go at home and go  to sleep at night i'm not stressed  because i've done something good that  day giving back has helped you survive  in more ways than one absolutely i am  walking in the steps  of the person that i want to be  running a purpose-driven business you  can still keep moving forward to reach  your goals this is a process that we're  going through and in knowing that that  allows us to have the resilience to just  keep going  lucy hones says in her book hope is the  fuel that fires us to move forward in  the world and tells us people need to  believe in hope that we have an  incredible capacity to cope even in our  darkest days and she highlights the idea  that part of resilience is being able to  shift your goal posts and realize you  can have many hopes saying the thing to  do is to ask yourself even though it  feels like all hope is lost what else  are you hoping for now and you know she  she told us that when she lost her  daughter her hopes became i hope my boys  live happy healthy lives i hope my  husband and i can be there for each  other i hope i can continue to do  meaningful work that behavioral mental  flexibility is so key she says yeah i  learned that you know that book man's  search for meaning by viktor frankl it's  one of my favorite books of all time he  talks about what it took for holocaust  survivors to get through something like  that it's exactly what you just said in  your tag moving the goal post and  believing that there will be a hopeful  day down the line the serenity to accept  the things you cannot change the courage  to change the things you can and the  wisdom to know the difference yeah the  words my mom told me from a very young  age stays with me always me too tara  thank you very much wow
 

